diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's premise is incorrect — Location C (PC11–PC14) is fully operational and there is no network anomaly.

A thorough end-to-end investigation was conducted. Router4, the site router for Location C, is running normally: its LAN-facing interface eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) is UP, switch4 bridges all ports correctly, and switch5/switch6 forward traffic to the PCs without issue. All four PCs (PC11–PC14) hold valid addresses in the 192.168.200.0/24 subnet and have a default route pointing to 192.168.200.1. FRR on router4 is running RIP version 2 and has a fully converged routing table, including learned routes to all remote site prefixes (172.16.10/24, 172.16.20/24, 192.168.1/24, 10.16.1/24, etc.).

Live connectivity tests confirm the absence of any fault: PC11 pinged its default gateway (192.168.200.1) with 0% loss, pinged PC12 on the same LAN with 0% loss, and successfully pinged PC4 at a remote location (172.16.10.4) through the WAN with 0% loss. PC13 and PC14 achieved identical results.

The user's assertion that "the site's router has obviously died" is a misdiagnosis. No device is faulty, no interface is down, and no routing adjacency is missing. The ticket should be closed as a false alarm; no corrective action is required.
=== END ===